WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.020
Here on It’s Lit!, we spend a lot of time
pontificating on the high canon of books:

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:09.860
Your Shakespeares, your Tolstoys, your…
erotic beast wars fanfiction.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.200
But today we’re craving something a little
lighter, a little fluffier… you know, novels

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.930
you pick up for the sake of just having something
quick-- your beach reads, your airport novels,

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.600
your Books of the Month.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.090
Books that, while you might never have to
read them for a seminar or a class or that

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:28.070
sweet clout, somehow manage to dominate most
water cooler discussions about literature.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:33.220
We’re talkin Your Dan Browns, your Jodi
Picoults, your Where the Crawdads Sing, seriously,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.660
how has the book managed to be on the top
of every best seller lists for so many months,

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.310
I don’t even KNOW what it’s about but
it’s my mortal enemy

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:46.810
These are your blockbuster books, the bestsellers
of the bestsellers--And whether or not you

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:51.110
read them or turn your nose up at them, for
better or worse, they are the tent poles that

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:52.600
support the publishing industry.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:53.600
Intro

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.330
So what do we mean when we say “best seller”?

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:02.520
Because, I’ll be real, it’s actually a
pretty nebulous term.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.750
Technically i am one - don’t read too much
into it

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.990
According to American literature professor
Sarah Garland: “The term “best-seller”

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:13.930
(or bestseller) as it is used by the press
is, in a sense, always hyperbole.

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.890
[...] To call a book a bestseller already
indicates that the book has come through a

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.600
series of decisions about whether to count
it.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.410
The bestseller represents the highest sales,
but,[...] the highest in sales where and when?

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:25.910
And for how long?”

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:29.160
It won’t surprise you to know that best-seller
lists, like the ones you see in The New York

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:34.550
Times or USA Today are fairly modern inventions,
but to really trace the origin of mass literature

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:39.390
we must go back and look at everybody’s
favorite fulcrum of literary history—the

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:40.820
printing press.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.370
So prior to its invention in the mid-15th
century, books were a precious commodity that

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:49.000
took serious time, effort, and money to create
and to buy.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.159
But even with the printing press bringing
down costs of bookmaking, most people, if

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:57.800
they could read and afford books, usually
owned mass-produced prayer books or heavily

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:04.250
spiritual titles—books like John Bunyan’s
Pilgrim Progress, a whip-cracking 100k words

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.979
of heavy religious metaphor about perdition
and hell.

00:02:06.979 --> 00:02:09.400
You know, a fun beach read.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.500
All this is to say that popular books then
were functional must-haves for the soul.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.280
And they were usually short.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.280
Very short.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.740
For production costs.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:22.180
Which ironically meant the Bible itself didn’t
sell particularly well until the 19th century.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:26.950
But in the 18th century, as the novel began
to take off, literary critics noticed almost

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:31.870
cult-like devotion to titles by authors like
Voltaire, whose Candide reportedly sold a

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:37.330
then-staggering 20,000 copies in its first
month, and Goethe’s The Sorrows of Young

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:44.960
Werther, which was so popular it had MERCHANDISE—from
prints, to porcelain, to perfume.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.810
Your move, Twilight promotional body spray.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:52.130
America didn’t get hip to this until the
mid 19th century, when publishers started

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:56.480
realizing they could gamify the industry by
following suit and by also publishing books

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.180
to the taste of audiences, and not necessarily
by what the intelligentsia deigned to disburse

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.340
to the rabble.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.819
And then the first real American best-sellers,
like Uncle Tom’s Cabin, start flooding bookstores.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:11.040
Books that dealt with highly topical, highly
emotional subject matter became must-reads

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.930
that everyone could get their hands on because
of highly strategic but bold publishing maneuvers.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:20.620
But unlike, say Uncle Tom’s Cabin, most
of these blockbusters eventually fell into

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:21.620
obscurity.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:26.120
The first published American best-seller list
appeared in 1895 in a new monthly magazine

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.000
called The Bookman.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.330
This magazine was imitating its London counterpart,
also called The Bookman, which had been publishing

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:37.700
a best-seller list for several years, and
used sales information from the leading bookstores

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.920
in sixteen cities (later extended to thirty
cities in the United States and Canada) to

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.620
gain information for its list of the six best-selling
titles for each town.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:52.319
And then, in 1931 came the absolute game-changer:
The New York Times Best Seller list.

00:03:52.319 --> 00:03:57.580
Initially, it only had a grand total of five
fiction and four non-fiction books on it,

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.390
and it only reflected numbers from the city
of New York itself.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:05.940
But by the 1950s, the controversial proprietary
ranking method had become THE list for tracking

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:11.340
book sales, and by the 1970s, bookstore chains
began using business models that highlighted

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.510
books with four words that have changed everything:
New York Times Bestseller.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:20.870
So despite competition from movies and television,
more people were reading novels than ever:

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:25.509
As literary critic Peter Swirski has highlighted,
“More than half of all the books ever published

00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:29.990
came out after the first hydrogen bomb; more
than half of all wordsmiths who ever put pen

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:32.689
to paper did so after the birth of TV…

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:37.220
The number of new titles released each year
in the United States quadrupled between 1950

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:38.220
and 1991.”

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:43.919
So now that we’re here, you might be wondering—how
does one get their book ON a best-seller list?

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:49.509
Does it have to be a masterpiece of emotion,
wit, and craft?

00:04:49.509 --> 00:04:50.509
na.

00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:54.259
Well if you’re thinking that publishers
decide who’s a bestseller before anything

00:04:54.259 --> 00:04:58.300
even gets printed… you’re kind of right.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:02.690
According to big-wig “lawyer-agent,” Morton
L. Janklow:

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:06.280
“Self-fulfilling prophecies play a large
part in book publishing.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:09.499
What makes a publisher decide that a first
printing should be a hundred thousand copies

00:05:09.499 --> 00:05:10.949
rather than sixty-five thousand?

00:05:10.949 --> 00:05:15.129
If a publisher buys a book for five thousand
dollars, it’ll be a miracle if anything

00:05:15.129 --> 00:05:16.360
happens to it.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.900
Because in the mind of the publisher it has
already failed.”

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:19.900
Well….

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:24.569
let’s create a hypothetical author -- his
name is Jimmy Squibbles, and he is a high

00:05:24.569 --> 00:05:30.200
profile Pocky beauty influencer, and he’s
ready to go ahead with his debut novel, Hype

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:31.200
House Bloodbath.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.490
Let’s put talent aside and suppose he has
a good literary agent who manages to net him

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:42.309
an advance from a big six … big five…
big four book publisher…. say $1.5 million

00:05:42.309 --> 00:05:43.309
dollars.

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:47.270
Jimmy Squibbles is in luck, because the higher
the advance, the more his publisher will push

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:52.120
hype house blood bath harder from a marketing
standpoint, which means more mainstream coverage,

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:53.830
which translates to more sales.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:58.259
Argues professor of sociology Laura J. Miller,
“The self-fulfilling nature of the list

00:05:58.259 --> 00:06:02.569
thus comes from the fact that the reading
public is constantly bombarded with information

00:06:02.569 --> 00:06:07.780
about, and opportunities and incentives to
buy books on the list—and buy they do.”

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:08.780
And this works!

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:09.960
Sort of.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.330
That push has to coincide with some sort of
demand in the market.

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:18.449
A good recent example of this is Jeanine Cummins’
American Dirt, which focuses on the topical

00:06:18.449 --> 00:06:25.190
topic of Central American migration to this
proud… land.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:29.389
American Dirt got a seven-figure advance from
its publisher, Flat Iron Books, and not one,

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:33.849
but TWO reviews in the New York Times.

00:06:33.849 --> 00:06:38.149
And while one was glowing, the other absolutely
trashed it.

00:06:38.149 --> 00:06:43.499
According to new york times critic Parul’s
sehgal: “I found myself flinching as I read,

00:06:43.499 --> 00:06:47.860
not from the perils the characters face, but
from the mauling the English language receives.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:52.419
Lydia’s expression “is one Luca has never
seen before, and he fears it might be permanent.

00:06:52.419 --> 00:06:56.129
It’s as if seven fishermen have cast their
hooks into her from different directions and

00:06:56.129 --> 00:06:57.479
they’re all pulling at once.

00:06:57.479 --> 00:07:01.849
One from the eyebrow, one from the lip, another
at the nose, one from the cheek.”

00:07:01.849 --> 00:07:03.050
Yes, of course.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:04.050
That expression.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:07.759
And once that type of negative publicity was
coupled with a huge outpouring of backlash

00:07:07.759 --> 00:07:12.020
regarding Cummins’ handling of a sensitive
subject matter while not being Latina herself,

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:14.199
well… who knows?

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:19.009
As in the case of American Dirt, backlash
can actually boost sales numbers a LOT.

00:07:19.009 --> 00:07:23.210
Some people were curious, and a LOT of people
wanted to support the book just to stick it

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.009
to the woke brigade that took issue with it.

00:07:26.009 --> 00:07:30.529
Controversy, legit or manufactured, is extremely
lucrative.

00:07:30.529 --> 00:07:34.199
American Dirt is one of the top selling titles
of 2020.

00:07:34.199 --> 00:07:35.349
So.

00:07:35.349 --> 00:07:41.360
One of the other big vehicles for pushing
a best-seller are book-club lists.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.710
Things like Oprah’s Book Club, for example,
which has single-handedly made many debut

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:51.639
or relatively unknown authors into stars—it
even made Leo Tolstoy’s 1877 novel Anna

00:07:51.639 --> 00:07:57.770
Karenina a best-seller in 2004! Fordham university
marketing professor Al Greco estimates that

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:02.650
sales of Oprah editions (aka books slapped
with Oprah’s book club stamp of approval

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:08.270
on the cover) have sold a total of 55 million
copies in the US alone, while book publicist

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:13.499
Patricia Eisemann has referred to Oprah’s
book club as “A Blessing from the book gods”.

00:08:13.499 --> 00:08:17.740
And while the list does tend to skew towards
a female audience—which probably explains

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:22.809
why noted pleasant person Jonathan Franzen
pitched a fit when his novel The Corrections

00:08:22.809 --> 00:08:27.180
was added to it—if Jimmy Squibbles and his
publisher don’t care about being marketed

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.580
to ~ women ~, then getting on her list is
a GOOD thing, Franz.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:35.770
There are, of course, less scrupulous means
of getting books into people’s hands.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:40.840
A few years ago, there was the controversy
of Handbook for Mortals author Lani Sarem

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:44.550
buying up her own books through third parties
and landing at the top of the NYT list for

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:47.900
YA fiction before being removed once she got
caught, or the Don JR.’s of the world who

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:48.900
purchase their own books in mass orders to
fudge best-seller numbers.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:52.400
This happens more often than you might think,
but usually with non fiction, and when it

00:08:52.400 --> 00:09:01.520
does the NYT puts a little dagger next to
it.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:05.970
Like hey, Ben Shapiro had a book come out
the very same day as mine and … look at

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:07.110
that dagger.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:11.250
But here’s the other, less acknowledged
caveat - being a best seller does not always

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:12.250
equate success.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:14.340
Let’s return to our friend, Jimmy Sqiubbles.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:19.090
He’s got that advance, he’s gotten mainstream
coverage, his novel, Hype House Blood Bath,

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:23.630
has been added to a bunch of book lists, and,
hey, let’s even say he already has a huge

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:27.380
built-in audience as a pocky beauty guru influencer.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:33.700
And it’s enough for him to get on both the
NYTs list for several weeks.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:34.700
Success?

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:35.700
Well… no.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:39.830
None of that means his book has found a staying
audience, nor does it necessarily mean he’s

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:41.370
earned back that huge advance.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:45.160
And unfortunately for Jimmy Squibbles, the
math is not on his side.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:51.240
With a standard boilerplate royalty rate and
an MSRP of, let’s say, $28, you might be

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:59.390
thinking, oh, he only needs to sell 1.5 million
divided by $28 = about 53k books - he’s

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:04.390
a Pocky influencer with 20 million subs and
9 billion instagram followers, that should

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:05.720
be easy right?

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:06.720
Well, no.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:13.620
That’s not how royalties work - with an
MSRP of $28, royalties are only a tiny percentage

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:18.380
of that, he actually needs to sell about 360k.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:22.820
And selling that many books, Thems some michelle
obama numbers

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:28.590
If Jimmy Squibbles sells 20-30k copies per
week for about 10 weeks - that is already

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:35.160
more than 99% of books will ever sell, and
also nowhere near enough to earn out his advance.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:40.250
And if interest in Hype House Bloodbath peters
out there, he’ll probably never earn it

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:41.250
out.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:47.010
So that book was technically a bestseller…
but not exactly a success.

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:51.540
And so like so many 19th century authors who
had their moment in the sun, Jimmy Squibbles

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:56.660
and Hype House Bloodbath fade into obscurity,
never to reach the heights of The Sun Also

00:10:56.660 --> 00:11:02.670
Rises or The Great Gatsby or Where The Crawdads
Si—SERIOUSLY WHO IS BUYING THIS BOOK?

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:03.990
MOM.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:04.990
STOP I-

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:08.360
If this all sounds labyrinthine or intimidating
for any aspiring author out there, well—there

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:09.830
is truth to that.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:13.940
Book publishing trends are finicky and wild,
with publishers hedging their bets on what

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:18.500
they think will sell, and not necessary with
any regards to a writer’s talent.

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:22.850
If Hype House Bloodbath becomes the next American
classic with a multi-million dollar movie

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:27.620
deal starring Logan Paul, you can be sure
that literary agents will be looking for manuscripts

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:31.870
that are similar to Hype House Bloodbath to
hop on that sweet gravy train.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:36.910
But it need not always be like this—it’s
not always large publishers that pick authors

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:37.910
out of obscurity.

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:41.580
There have been many notable titles that have
found larger audiences via a growing indie

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:44.440
publishing industry or through less orthodox
means.

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:48.450
Margaret Atwood’s first published book of
poetry, Double Persephone, was published as

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:52.720
a pamphlet by a small press in 1961, winning
her an EJ Pratt Medal.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:56.730
Andy Weir’s The Martian, which sat on the
Best Seller list for 76 weeks (19 of those

00:11:56.730 --> 00:12:04.760
at number one!) started as a series of blog
posts that he compiled, self-published, and

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:06.170
sold for 99 cents.

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:10.100
Lisa Genova’s Still Alice was originally
self-published on a digital self-publishing

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:14.950
platform iUniverse, with copies literally
being sold out of the trunk of her car.

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:18.750
And Fifty Shades of Grey… well… uh…
we don’t need to talk about that.

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:22.110
Point being that getting a book published
is incredibly difficult, to say nothing about

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:23.800
turning it into a “hit.”

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:29.110
And this is not to dunk on authors who do
make it on the list or suggest there is no

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:33.040
merit to a best-selling novel—for those
who do make it, it’s often the result of

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:38.340
years of sculpting away at an idea, hundreds
of hours of revising and revisiting and completely

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:41.570
rewriting out of a labor of love or devotion
to storytelling.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:45.430
But more often it’s just who the publisher
thinks should be there.

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:58.490
Sorry bout it.

